hv build options -- what each -U/-D does and why
===============================================

The list is HV_OPTIONS in config.mk. options.mk renames it to
QEMU_EXTRA_OPTIONS and hands it to whichever toolchain runs -- Sun's
EXTRA_OPTIONS= or HV_EXTRA_OPTS= to gnu/hv-opts.sh -- once, not copied a
second time. Override the whole list on the command line:

    make hv_reset HV_OPTIONS='-UFOR_ZEUS -UCONFIG_LEGIONBCOPY ...'

Every -U here except -UDEBUG_LEGION came from a live crash under QEMU,
confirmed against the hv source. None was flipped on suspicion.


-UFOR_ZEUS
    reset.s reads membase/memsize/HVPD/HV from a table only Sun's Legion
    simulator fills in. Left defined, the hv jumps to physical 0x20.

-UCONFIG_LEGIONBCOPY
    bcopy/xcopy become `ta 0x75`, a magic trap only Legion intercepts. Left
    defined: MAXTL trap storm.

-UDEBUG_LEGION
    defines LEGION_MAGICTRAP_PABCOPY, used under CONFIG_LEGIONBCOPY above.
    Inert on its own for this source set; cleared to match the validated
    recipe.

-URESETCONFIG_BROKENTICK
    main.s reads JBUS registers (JBI_PERF_CTL, CLK_BASE/CLK_DIV_REG) to
    calibrate %tick. QEMU models none of them.

-UCONFIG_HVUART
    Off by default; HV_UART=yes or ./configure --enable-hv-uart removes it.
    puts()/uart_init() are the hv's own early debug console, polling an
    NS16550 at FPGA_UART_BASE (0xfff0c2c000). Sun's legion variant defines
    CONFIG_HVUART itself; it is this list that turns it off. Upstream QEMU has
    no device there -- that address is unmapped in the niagara machine, not
    even the sun4v-iob stub -- so puts()'s LSR-THRE poll can spin forever and
    the hv hangs before its first character, with no diagnostic. The default
    build targets upstream QEMU, so this is off.

    To get the hv debug console back, build a QEMU that models the device
    (the niagara-hvuart-console patch in
    https://github.com/unix0cc/qemu-experimental-patches, devel/), build the
    firmware with HV_UART=yes (make HV_UART=yes, or ./configure
    --enable-hv-uart), and give QEMU a second -serial for it
    (-serial mon:stdio -serial file:hv-console.log). A set built this way
    hangs on a QEMU without the patch.


Sun defaults left as they are, worth knowing about
--------------------------------------------------
-DNIAGARA_ERRATUM_39 and -DNIAGARA_ERRATUM_43 are not part of the override
list above -- they come from Sun's own Makefile.platform
($(DESUPPORT_NIAGARA_1x)PLAT_OPTIONS += ..., always on since
DESUPPORT_NIAGARA_1x is commented out), unconditionally on for both
toolchains. Listed here only because they explain a QEMU-side gap, not
because this build sets them.

    Erratum #39 (hcall_cpu.s, plat_halt_strand): guards against real
    Niagara 1.x silicon, which could not reliably wake a halted strand on
    an interrupt. Checks %hver's major field; on a part that looks like
    1.x it skips the real halt write entirely rather than risk it. QEMU
    never assigned %hver (always read 0 == "oldest possible part"), so this
    guard always fired regardless of which CPU was actually modelled -- see
    the niagara-strand-halt-erratum-39 patch in
    https://github.com/unix0cc/qemu-experimental-patches, devel/.

    Erratum #43 (traptable.h, VECINTR): unrelated to halt -- a membar #Sync
    inserted before the vectored-interrupt trap reads ASI_INTR_UDB_R, to
    avoid a different silicon bug (partial load hanging the thread right
    after a Block_Init_Store).


Not in the list, on purpose
---------------------------
-DT1_FPGA is NOT set. With it, scrub_all_memory (main.s:1483) compiles in a
block that spreads memory-scrub work across strands and busy-waits on a
per-strand bitmask that never clears under QEMU's max_cpus=1. Without it the
block is gone and the strand broadcast is a plain bounded loop, so Sun's
reset.s boots unpatched.
